Step 1
Refactor the duplicated Scan logic.

Create a helper inside

internal/review/handlers.go
func (h *handler) loadPage(r *http.Request) (reviewmodel.Page, error) {
	min := 100

	if s := r.URL.Query().Get("min"); s != "" {
		if v, err := strconv.Atoi(s); err == nil && v > 0 {
			min = v
		}
	}

	current := 0

	if s := r.URL.Query().Get("current"); s != "" {
		if v, err := strconv.Atoi(s); err == nil && v >= 0 {
			current = v
		}
	}

	result, err := Scan(h.imageDir, min)
	if err != nil {
		return reviewmodel.Page{}, err
	}

	if len(result.Images) == 0 {
		current = 0
	} else if current >= len(result.Images) {
		current = len(result.Images) - 1
	}

	return reviewmodel.Page{
		Result:  result,
		Current: current,
	}, nil
}
Notice that this also clamps invalid indexes.

That saves us from crashes later.

Step 2
Your existing

func (h *handler) home(...)
becomes tiny.

page, err := h.loadPage(r)
if err != nil {
    ...
}

err = reviewtpl.Review(page).Render(...)
No Scan code remains here.

Step 3
Add another handler.

func (h *handler) content(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {

	page, err := h.loadPage(r)
	if err != nil {
		h.log.Error("scan failed", "err", err)
		http.Error(w, "scan failed", http.StatusInternalServerError)
		return
	}

	err = reviewtpl.ReviewContent(page).Render(r.Context(), w)
	if err != nil {
		h.log.Error("render failed", "err", err)
	}
}
Almost identical to home().

The only difference is

Review()
vs

ReviewContent()
Step 4
Register the endpoint.

Current

r.Get("/", h.home)
becomes

r.Get("/", h.home)
r.Get("/content", h.content)
Nothing else changes.

What should work
Open

http://localhost:8080/review?min=200
Exactly the same page.

Then open

http://localhost:8080/review/content?min=200
You should not see the page header or layout.

You should see only

review-panel
This is an excellent debugging endpoint.

Why this is important
When HTMX does

hx-get="/review/content?current=12"
the browser literally performs the exact request you just tested manually.

That makes debugging easy because every HTMX request is also a normal URL you can visit directly.

Small improvement
While you're editing loadPage(), I'd also preserve the current min value automatically in the page model (which it already does through Result.MinSize). That means every subsequent HTMX request can include both current and min, so changing images won't accidentally reset the filter.

If this compiles and /review/content?... returns just the review panel, we're ready for Commit 4, where the Previous/Next buttons become live HTMX navigation with only a few template changes.



