Fantastic. You're at the point where the architecture is in place. Now let's verify the HTMX behavior and, if necessary, finish the last wiring.

Step 1: Is HTMX loaded?
Open your browser's Developer Tools and run:

typeof htmx
You should get:

object
If you get:

undefined
then HTMX isn't being loaded, and the buttons will perform no action.

Verify your base layout includes:

<script src="/static/js/htmx.min.js"></script>
just before </body>.

Step 2: Inspect the rendered button
Right-click the Next button and choose Inspect.

You should see something similar to:

<button
    class="button is-link"
    hx-get="/review/content?current=1&min=200"
    hx-target="#review-panel"
    hx-swap="outerHTML">
    Next →
</button>
If those hx-* attributes are missing, the template wasn't regenerated or the wrong template is being rendered.

Step 3: Watch the Network tab
Open the Network tab in DevTools.

Click Next.

You should see a request like:

GET /review/content?current=1&min=200
There are three possibilities:

A. No request at all
HTMX isn't loaded.

B. Request returns 404
The /review/content route isn't mounted correctly.

C. Request returns 200
Perfect. The server is working.

Step 4: Test the endpoint directly
Visit:

http://localhost:8080/review/content?current=1&min=200
You should see only the review panel (no page header or refresh form), and it should display the second image if you have more than one.

If it always shows the first image, we'll inspect ReviewContent() or Page.Image() next.

One thing I strongly recommend next
Once the HTMX navigation is confirmed, I'd make a small usability improvement before adding keyboard shortcuts.

Instead of only showing "Previous" and "Next", let's display something like:

← Previous        Image 5 / 143        Next →
and disable the buttons at the ends of the list (which your model already supports with HasPrev() and HasNext()).

After that, the next feature I'd add is keyboard navigation:

← moves to the previous image

→ moves to the next image

Because you've already split the page into a ReviewContent partial and added the content endpoint, that enhancement will only require a tiny amount of JavaScript or HTMX event handling and won't require any changes to the server.


