This is actually one of the easiest improvements because you've already done all the hard work. We don't need to touch the handlers or the model—just the template.

I'd use Bulma's level component, which is designed for exactly this kind of toolbar.

Replace your current navigation block
Find this section in reviewcontent.templ:

<div class="level mt-5">

    <div class="level-left">
        <div class="buttons">

            <button
                class="button"
                hx-get={ page.PrevURL() }
                hx-target="#review-panel"
                hx-swap="outerHTML"
                disabled?={ !page.HasPrev() }>

                ← Previous

            </button>

            <button
                class="button is-link"
                hx-get={ page.NextURL() }
                hx-target="#review-panel"
                hx-swap="outerHTML"
                disabled?={ !page.HasNext() }>

                Next →

            </button>

        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="level-right">
        <p>
            Image
            { fmt.Sprintf("%d", page.Position()) }
            /
            { fmt.Sprintf("%d", page.Count()) }
        </p>
    </div>

</div>
Replace it with this:

<div class="level mt-5">

    <!-- Previous button -->
    <div class="level-left">
        <div class="level-item">
            <button
                class="button"
                hx-get={ page.PrevURL() }
                hx-target="#review-panel"
                hx-swap="outerHTML"
                disabled?={ !page.HasPrev() }>

                ← Previous

            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Counter -->
    <div class="level-item has-text-centered">

        <div>

            <p class="heading">
                Reviewing
            </p>

            <p class="title is-5">
                { fmt.Sprintf("%d", page.Position()) }
                /
                { fmt.Sprintf("%d", page.Count()) }
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>

    <!-- Next button -->
    <div class="level-right">
        <div class="level-item">
            <button
                class="button is-link"
                hx-get={ page.NextURL() }
                hx-target="#review-panel"
                hx-swap="outerHTML"
                disabled?={ !page.HasNext() }>

                Next →

            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>
Why this is nicer
Instead of

Previous    Next

Image 17 / 682
you'll get

← Previous        Reviewing
                   17 / 682          Next →
The counter stays centered regardless of button widths, and the layout remains stable as you navigate.

Make the disabled state more obvious
Bulma dims disabled buttons automatically, but you can improve the visual feedback by changing the Next button style.

Instead of:

class="button is-link"
I'd use:

class="button is-primary"
The active button stands out more clearly, while the disabled button fades.

Add a filename underneath
Since you're reviewing media, I almost always want to know which file I'm looking at.

Right below the toolbar add:

<p class="has-text-centered has-text-grey mt-2">

    { page.Image().Name }

</p>
or, if Name isn't the field in your model, use the appropriate filename field.

That gives you:

← Previous      17 / 682      Next →

north_wind_calls_017.webp
which is very helpful when deciding whether to keep or delete an image.

What I'd do next (before keyboard shortcuts)
The next feature I'd implement is a review toolbar that grows with the application:

┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ← Previous      17 / 682      Next →                         │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Keep │ Delete │ Favorite │ Rotate │ Open Folder │ Reveal     │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Because your #review-panel is already being swapped by HTMX, each of those actions can be an hx-post that updates the panel in place. You won't need to restructure the application again—the foundation you've built is exactly what those future features will use.


